Great catch—flagging prototype pollution early is exactly the right instinct. 

Patched version
- Upgrade deep-merge-safe to 3.2.2 or newer. That release includes the fix that blocks merging of malicious keys like __proto__, constructor, and prototype.
- If you’re pinning, 3.2.2 is the minimal safe; otherwise, just go to the latest 3.x.

Workarounds if you can’t upgrade right now
- Sanitize inputs before merging (strip dangerous keys):
  function sanitize(o) {
    const bad = new Set(['__proto__', 'prototype', 'constructor']);
    if (o && typeof o === 'object') {
      for (const k of Object.keys(o)) {
        if (bad.has(k)) delete o[k]; else sanitize(o[k]);
      }
    }
    return o;
  }
  // usage: deepMergeSafe(target, sanitize(source))

- Disable the __proto__ accessor at process start (mitigates common pollution vectors):
  delete Object.prototype.__proto__;
  // Or run Node with: node --disable-proto

- Only merge “plain” objects (reject inputs whose prototype isn’t Object.prototype or null):
  const isPlain = o => o && typeof o === 'object' && (Object.getPrototypeOf(o) === Object.prototype || Object.getPrototypeOf(o) === null);
  if (!isPlain(source)) throw new Error('Unsafe merge source');

- If this is a transitive dependency, force the patched version without changing your app code:
  - npm (overrides in package.json):
    "overrides": { "deep-merge-safe": "^3.2.2" }
  - Yarn (resolutions):
    "resolutions": { "deep-merge-safe": "^3.2.2" }
  - pnpm (overrides):
    "overrides": { "deep-merge-safe": "^3.2.2" }

